    linkStyle 3 stroke:#a76bee,stroke-width:3px In LightnerX, the application connects components through callbacks: the network delivers bytes, and MessageInterpreter interprets those bytes according to the direction of communication. When sending, lightnerx::network::Target lets you select a connection, a group, or a broadcast with or without exceptions. Navigation ¶ Setup First steps Communication and the INER protocol API reference C++17 compact types Migration and validation Server example ¶ This example receives notifications from the client and sends the text Hello, World! in events to registered connections. The snippets below make up the complete program at the end of the README; the statements belong inside the main function, after the includes shown in the complete code. Create the components ¶ The manager maps handles to internal client IDs. Register ID 47 so that the interpreter recognizes notifications sent by the client in this example. using namespace std::chrono_literals;
namespace network = lightnerx::network;
namespace communication = lightnerx::communication;

network::Server server;
network::ConnectionManager connections{30, 30};
communication::MessageInterpreter interpreter;
communication::NotificationRegistry notifications;
notifications.registerId(47); Track connections ¶ The callbacks keep the manager up to date. If registering a connection fails, the server requests its disconnection. server.addClientConnectedCallback([&](HSteamNetConnection connection) {
    if (!connections.add(connection)) {
        if (const auto result = server.disconnectConnection(connection, false); !result) {
            std::cout << "Failed to disconnect: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
        }
    }
});

server.addClientDisconnectedCallback([&](HSteamNetConnection connection) {
    if (const auto id = connections.getClientId(connection)) {
        connections.remove(*id);
    }
}); Receive messages ¶ Forward the received bytes to obedientInterpret . The interpreter's callback delivers the message and the internal client ID. In this example, the payload is text and is read during the callback. server.addClientReceivedMessageCallback(
    [&](lightnerx::compact::span<const std::byte> bytes, HSteamNetConnection connection) {
        interpreter.obedientInterpret(bytes, notifications, connection, connections);
    }
);

interpreter.addObedientInterpretedMessageCallback(
    [](communication::Message message, std::uint16_t clientId) {
        const auto text = message.payload.empty() ? std::string_view{} : std::string_view{
            reinterpret_cast<const char*>(message.payload.data()), message.payload.size()
        };
        std::cout << "Client " << clientId << ": " << text << '\n';
    }
); Start the server ¶ Start listening on port 41333 , with an established connection timeout of 3000 ms, and reserve one slot for the batcher's pending message. if (const auto result = server.start(41333, 3000); !result) {
    std::cout << "Failed to start: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
    return 1;
}
network::initMessagesBatcher(1); Prepare an event ¶ Convert the text to bytes and include the header and payload in the total size. For Event , the size is shifted one bit to the left. Type 990 is chosen by the application. const std::string_view greeting = "Hello, World!";
const auto payload = lightnerx::compact::as_bytes(lightnerx::compact::span{greeting});
const communication::Event event{
    // The least significant bit distinguishes Event from Response.
    static_cast<std::uint16_t>((sizeof(communication::Event) + payload.size()) << 1),
    990
}; Update and send ¶ update() processes the network. The converter creates an OutboundMessage , which is passed directly to queueMessage . Target::broadcast() selects the connections registered at flush time. while (true) {
    if (const auto result = server.update(); !result) {
        std::cout << "Failed to update: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
        break;
    }
    if (const auto result = network::queueMessage(communication::event2OutboundMessage(
            event, payload, network::Target::broadcast())); !result) {
        std::cout << "Failed to queue: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
        break;
    }
    if (const auto result = network::flushAll(connections); !result) {
        std::cout << "Failed to send: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
        break;
    }
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(3s);
} queueMessage takes ownership of the message, including when it returns an error; the application does not call Release() afterward. flushAll processes the queue and releases the messages. The three-second interval is a choice made for this example; the application defines its own update rate. Shut down the server ¶ After exiting the loop due to an error, close the server. Returning 1 signals an exit due to failure in this example. server.close();
return 1; Client example ¶ The client connects to the server at 127.0.0.1:41333 , prints the received events, and sends a notification with the same text. Run the server and client in separate processes. Create the components ¶ The empty manager satisfies the flushAll signature. Sending to the server individually uses the client's connection handle directly. using namespace std::chrono_literals;
namespace network = lightnerx::network;
namespace communication = lightnerx::communication;

network::Client client;
network::ConnectionManager connections{0, 0}; // Required by flush, without broadcast.
communication::MessageInterpreter interpreter; Receive messages from the server ¶ Forward the bytes to authorityInterpret , which interprets messages from the Authority. The callback below prints the text payload agreed upon by these examples and handles empty payloads. client.addServerReceivedMessageCallback([&](lightnerx::compact::span<const std::byte> bytes) {
    interpreter.authorityInterpret(bytes);
});

interpreter.addAuthorityInterpretedMessageCallback([](communication::Message message) {
    const auto text = message.payload.empty() ? std::string_view{} : std::string_view{
        reinterpret_cast<const char*>(message.payload.data()), message.payload.size()
    };
    std::cout << "Server: " << text << '\n';
}); The callbacks use the bytes during the call. To retain a received payload, copy its bytes into application storage before returning. Start and connect ¶ Initialize the client, request a connection to the server, and reserve space in the batcher. The connection will be tracked during calls to update() . if (const auto result = client.start(); !result) {
    std::cout << "Failed to start: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
    return 1;
}
if (const auto result = client.connect("127.0.0.1", 41333); !result) {
    std::cout << "Failed to connect: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
    return 1;
}
network::initMessagesBatcher(1); Prepare a notification ¶ Notification uses the total size directly, without bit shifting. ID 47 corresponds to the registration made on the server. const std::string_view greeting = "Hello, World!";
const auto payload = lightnerx::compact::as_bytes(lightnerx::compact::span{greeting});
const communication::Notification notification{
    static_cast<std::uint16_t>(sizeof(communication::Notification) + payload.size()),
    47 // The server must register this ID in NotificationRegistry.
}; Update and send to the server ¶ Process up to 32 messages per update and send when isConnected() indicates an established connection. Target::single selects the server's handle. while (true) {
    if (const auto result = client.update(32); !result) {
        std::cout << "Failed to update: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
        break;
    }
    if (client.isConnected()) {
        if (const auto result = network::queueMessage(communication::notification2OutboundMessage(
                notification, payload, network::Target::single(client.getServerHConn()))); !result) {
            std::cout << "Failed to queue: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
            break;
        }
        if (const auto result = network::flushAll(connections); !result) {
            std::cout << "Failed to send: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
            break;
        }
    }
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(3000ms);
} Shut down the client ¶ When exiting due to an error, the Client destructor closes the connection and shuts down the network. Returning 1 indicates the failure that interrupted the example. return 1; // The Client destructor closes the connection and shuts down the network. Complete code ¶ The programs below bring together the snippets presented above. The text payload, IDs, loop, and intervals are choices made by the consuming application. Complete server ¶ #include <steam/steamnetworkingtypes.h>

#include <chrono>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>
#include <thread>
#include <lightnerx/compact/span.hpp>

#include <lightnerx/network/server.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/network/connection_manager.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/network/message_batch.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/network/target.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/communication/message.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/communication/notification.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/communication/event.hpp>

int main() {
    using namespace std::chrono_literals;
    namespace network = lightnerx::network;
    namespace communication = lightnerx::communication;

    network::Server server;
    network::ConnectionManager connections{30, 30};
    communication::MessageInterpreter interpreter;
    communication::NotificationRegistry notifications;
    notifications.registerId(47);

    server.addClientConnectedCallback([&](HSteamNetConnection connection) {
        if (!connections.add(connection)) {
            if (const auto result = server.disconnectConnection(connection, false); !result) {
                std::cout << "Failed to disconnect: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
            }
        }
    });

    server.addClientDisconnectedCallback([&](HSteamNetConnection connection) {
        if (const auto id = connections.getClientId(connection)) {
            connections.remove(*id);
        }
    });

    server.addClientReceivedMessageCallback(
        [&](lightnerx::compact::span<const std::byte> bytes, HSteamNetConnection connection) {
            interpreter.obedientInterpret(bytes, notifications, connection, connections);
        }
    );

    interpreter.addObedientInterpretedMessageCallback(
        [](communication::Message message, std::uint16_t clientId) {
            const auto text = message.payload.empty() ? std::string_view{} : std::string_view{
                reinterpret_cast<const char*>(message.payload.data()), message.payload.size()
            };
            std::cout << "Client " << clientId << ": " << text << '\n';
        }
    );

    if (const auto result = server.start(41333, 3000); !result) {
        std::cout << "Failed to start: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
        return 1;
    }
    network::initMessagesBatcher(1);

    const std::string_view greeting = "Hello, World!";
    const auto payload = lightnerx::compact::as_bytes(lightnerx::compact::span{greeting});
    const communication::Event event{
        // The least significant bit distinguishes Event from Response.
        static_cast<std::uint16_t>((sizeof(communication::Event) + payload.size()) << 1),
        990
    };

    while (true) {
        if (const auto result = server.update(); !result) {
            std::cout << "Failed to update: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
            break;
        }
        if (const auto result = network::queueMessage(communication::event2OutboundMessage(
                event, payload, network::Target::broadcast())); !result) {
            std::cout << "Failed to queue: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
            break;
        }
        if (const auto result = network::flushAll(connections); !result) {
            std::cout << "Failed to send: " << result.error().message() << '\n';
            break;
        }
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(3s);
    }

    server.close();
    return 1;
} Complete client ¶ #include <steam/steamnetworkingtypes.h>

