} connect() initiates the connection, and update() processes network callbacks and available messages. isConnected() can be used to query the state observed by the client. Execution model ¶ The server.update() and client.update() methods are called by the application. They can run on the main thread, on a dedicated thread, or within the consuming project's existing execution loop. The library does not require a specific number of threads or a fixed interval between updates. Run these server and client examples in separate processes: exactly one started wrapper owns networking in each process. Wrappers cannot be copied or moved. Receive callbacks run during update() , and interpretation callbacks run on the thread that calls the interpreter. When sharing components across threads, handle synchronization in the application. For the batcher, serialize initialization, queuing, clearing and flush calls, which operate on the same queue. Callback exceptions propagate after resource cleanup; catch them around update() if the application intends to continue. See callback behavior for supported reentrant operations. Batch sending ¶ With the network initialized by Server::start() , Client::start() , or lightnerx::network::networkInit() , reserve space in the batcher before queuing the first message. Include <lightnerx/network/message_batch.hpp> and call: lightnerx::network::initMessagesBatcher(64); The destination is defined by lightnerx::network::Target . The following function creates an Event , routes it to a group, and sends the queue. It assumes the initialization above and returns the result of queuing or flushing to the caller. The same flow applies to the other message converters. #include "steam/steamnetworkingtypes.h"

#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>
#include <system_error>
#include <lightnerx/compact/span.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/compact/expected.hpp>

#include <lightnerx/communication/event.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/communication/message.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/network/connection_manager.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/network/message_batch.hpp>
#include <lightnerx/network/target.hpp>

lightnerx::compact::expected<void, std::error_code> sendEventToGroup(
    lightnerx::compact::span<const std::byte> payload,
    std::vector<HSteamNetConnection> receivers,
    const lightnerx::network::ConnectionManager& connections
) {
    const auto totalSize = sizeof(lightnerx::communication::Event) + payload.size();
    if (totalSize > 0x7fff) {
        return lightnerx::compact::unexpected(std::make_error_code(std::errc::message_size));
    }

    const lightnerx::communication::Event event{
        static_cast<std::uint16_t>(totalSize << 1),
        47
    };

    auto outbound = lightnerx::communication::event2OutboundMessage(
        event,
        payload,
        lightnerx::network::Target::group(std::move(receivers))
    );

    const auto queued = lightnerx::network::queueMessage(std::move(outbound));

    if (!queued) {
        return queued;
    }

    return lightnerx::network::flushAll(connections);
} queueMessage receives the OutboundMessage created by the converter and takes ownership of the message and its destination, without reallocating the message or copying its bytes. If the queue is full, the call releases the message immediately and returns an error. If queuing is accepted, flushAll releases the message after the send attempt. Pass an existing object with std::move(outbound) or pass the converter's result directly to queueMessage . After the call, do not access, release, or requeue the consumed message, including in the event of an error. Since message is a raw pointer, std::move does not automatically set it to null in the original object. The header and payload provided to the converter remain owned by the application. With handles obtained by the client or through the server's connection callbacks, the available destinations are: auto oneClient = lightnerx::network::Target::single(connection);
auto selectedClients = lightnerx::network::Target::group({firstConnection, secondConnection});
auto everyone = lightnerx::network::Target::broadcast();
auto everyoneElse = lightnerx::network::Target::broadcast({connectionToSkip}); These calls only describe destinations. You can queue several messages and run flushAll(connections) at the end of an application step; the immediate flush shown in the function is a choice made for the example. Next steps ¶ See Communication and the INER protocol to understand headers, destinations, and sending flows. See API reference for networking components, messages, Target , and the batcher.

