Communication and the INER protocol ¶ The examples use the C++17 types in lightnerx::compact for byte views and error results. INER and LightnerX ¶ INER is a semantic protocol. It defines the meaning and organization of messages, but does not define sockets, addressing, threads, or the transmission mechanism. LightnerX implements the protocol's message types and provides networking components based on GameNetworkingSockets. The division is: Responsibility INER LightnerX Meaning of Event, Response, Intention, and Notification Yes Represented by C++ types Message identification Yes MBT::type , correlationId , and status fields Header encoding Yes Packed structures and converters Sockets and connections No Server , Client , and GameNetworkingSockets Execution model No Chosen by the application Roles ¶ Authority ¶ The Authority controls internal states. It receives Intention and Notification from the Obedient and sends Event and Response . In LightnerX, the server typically interprets received messages with: interpreter.obedientInterpret(bytes, registry, connection, connections); Obedient ¶ The Obedient sends intentions to the Authority and receives information about occurrences and results. In LightnerX, the client typically interprets received messages with: interpreter.authorityInterpret(bytes); Message types ¶ Choose the message type according to its purpose and direction. In a typical client-server application, the server acts as the Authority and the client as the Obedient. Message type Direction When to use it Examples Intention Client → Server Request an action that the Authority must evaluate. Move a player, fire a weapon, use an item, join a match. Notification Client → Server Report information without requesting a change to the Authority's internal state. Report measured ping/latency, FPS, or client diagnostics. Event Server → Client Communicate an occurrence or state update from the Authority. A player joined, an entity moved, a projectile spawned, a match started. Response Server → Client Return the outcome of a specific Intention, linked by its correlationId . Movement approved, firing denied because ammunition is insufficient, an action partially applied, or processing failed. These examples describe application-defined messages; LightnerX does not provide built-in gameplay commands or telemetry formats. A Response reports an Intention's outcome, while an Event communicates an occurrence to the relevant clients. Event ¶ Event communicates an occurrence from the Authority. Its header contains: BaseProtocol::size ; MBT::type , used as type_id . The payload, when present, follows the header. Response ¶ Response relates the result to an intention through correlationId and contains a status . The statuses are exposed by constants in the <lightnerx/communication/response.hpp> header: Constant Value Meaning Type used by LightnerX APPROVED_RESPONSE 0 intention fully approved Response DENIED_RESPONSE 1 intention denied UnsuccessfulResponse PARTIALLY_APPROVED_RESPONSE 2 intention partially applied UnsuccessfulResponse FAILURE_RESPONSE 3 internal error during processing Response A failure does not necessarily mean that the intention was denied; the application may decide to try again. UnsuccessfulResponse adds reasonCode to the header of denied or partially approved responses. For partial approval, the meaning of the payload is defined by the application. Intention ¶ Intention requests that the Authority perform an action. Its header contains: BaseProtocol::size ; MBT::type ; correlationId . The intention's payload follows this header. Notification ¶ Notification communicates information to the Authority without requiring state validation, since it does not request a change to the Authority's internal states. Its header contains size and type , followed by the optional payload. On the server, NotificationRegistry specifies which type_id values should be interpreted as Notification : #include <unordered_set>
#include <cstdint>
#include <lightnerx/communication/notification.hpp>

void registerNotifications(lightnerx::communication::NotificationRegistry& registry) {
    registry.registerId(47);
    registry.registerIds(std::unordered_set<std::uint16_t>{99, 100});
} The type_id values are defined by the application. There is no universal table of INER types. When receiving messages from the Obedient, registered IDs identify notifications; the others identify intentions. To send notifications, use notification2OutboundMessage . Binary representation ¶ Header structures are packed without additional padding using #pragma pack(push, 1) . The converters copy fields in the machine's native representation; participants must use a compatible representation for these fields and agree on the payload format. Messages from the Authority ¶ The first field uses a combined value: the least significant bit indicates whether the message is a Response , and the remaining fifteen bits represent the size. The table describes the recommended formats for emission: approved, denied and failure responses have no payload; partial approval may carry application-defined bytes. Message Header Recommended content when sending Event 2 bytes of combined size + 2 bytes of type variable payload Response 2 bytes of combined size + 2 bytes of correlationId + 1 byte of status no payload for the statuses represented by Response UnsuccessfulResponse Response structure + 2 bytes of reasonCode variable payload for a partial response When constructing the header, the combined value takes the form: Event:    (total_size << 1) | 0
Response: (total_size << 1) | 1 The interpreter retrieves the size with base >> 1 and distinguishes Event from Response with base & 1 . In the headers delivered to the callback, protocol.size already contains the decoded size, without the type bit. Reception currently tolerates additional bytes in responses when the declared total matches the supplied span and the selected header is complete. For approved and failure responses, the interpreter ignores those bytes and delivers an empty payload. For denied and partially approved responses, it delivers all bytes after UnsuccessfulResponse , even though the recommended denied-response format has no payload. protocol.size still includes the complete received message, including any ignored bytes; it can therefore exceed the header size plus the delivered payload size. This reception behavior does not change the recommended emission formats. Converters copy the supplied payload without enforcing status-specific payload rules. Messages from the Obedient ¶ In messages from the Obedient, size directly represents the total message size. Message Header Content Notification 2 bytes of size + 2 bytes of type variable payload Intention 2 bytes of size + 2 bytes of type + 2 bytes of correlationId variable payload The specified size must include the header and payload. Each interpretation call must receive exactly one complete message: the decoded total must equal the supplied span and the header must be complete, otherwise no callback is delivered. Authority messages are limited to 32767 bytes and Obedient messages to 65535 bytes. The interpreter does not split concatenated messages or buffer partial frames. Unknown response statuses are ignored. The converters copy the header and payload into a SteamNetworkingMessage_t ; they do not automatically construct the semantic value of the size field. They throw std::bad_alloc if GNS allocation fails and std::length_error if the allocation size cannot be represented. Identifiers ¶ type_id identifies the semantic type defined by the application and appears in the code as MBT::type . correlationId relates a response to its corresponding intention. The ID received in the server's interpreted message callback is the internal identifier assigned by ConnectionManager . This internal ID belongs to the connection management implementation and is not an INER protocol field. The name originId does not designate a protocol field either. Network destinations use HSteamNetConnection handles, which are distinct from internal IDs and semantic identifiers. Network destinations ¶ lightnerx::network::Target , declared in the <lightnerx/network/target.hpp> header, defines where a queued message will be sent. The static factories create the destination description: Factory Destination Target::single(connection) a specific connection Target::group(connections) all connections in the provided vector Target::broadcast() all connections registered in ConnectionManager Target::broadcast(exceptions) all connections registered in ConnectionManager , except the specified handles Target::group stores the explicit selection of handles. Broadcast queries the ConnectionManager passed to flushAll at flush time, using the mappings maintained by the application. Its exceptions are also HSteamNetConnection handles. Receiving flow ¶ Network -> Server callback -> obedientInterpret -> application callback
Network -> Client callback -> authorityInterpret -> application callback MessageInterpreter delivers a lightnerx::communication::Message whose header contains the concrete type in a std::variant and whose payload is a lightnerx::compact::span<const std::byte> . Callbacks run during the interpretation call. The header is delivered by value, but the span only references the received bytes. In the flow with Server or Client , these bytes are released after the receive callbacks. To retain the payload, copy its bytes during the callback; copying only Message keeps a reference to the same buffer. Sending flow ¶ The usual flow is: Initialize the network and call initMessagesBatcher(capacity) before queuing the first message. Construct the header with the total message size. Create a lightnerx::network::Target . Use the corresponding converter to obtain a lightnerx::network::OutboundMessage . Pass the object to queueMessage(std::move(outbound)) , transferring ownership of the message and destination. Check the queuing result and call flushAll(connectionManager) at the time chosen by the application. Releasing the message becomes the batcher's responsibility, including when queuing returns an error. Example of an Event from the Authority with one byte of payload defined by the application. The function assumes that the network and batcher have been initialized and supports broadcast with or without exceptions: #include "steam/steamnetworkingtypes.h"

