); initMessagesBatcher reserves space in the batcher's shared queue. Call it before queuing, with the number of messages you want to be able to keep pending; this reservation does not empty an existing queue. queueMessage receives an OutboundMessage by value and moves it into the queue. The message allocated by the converter is retained, without allocating another message or copying the bytes. If the reserved capacity is occupied, it releases the received message and returns std::errc::not_enough_memory . If message.message is null, it returns std::errc::invalid_argument without queuing. flushAll processes each message separately by sending it to an individual connection, a group, or a broadcast. ConnectionManager is a required argument and provides the broadcast recipients. The function releases all internal messages and empties the queue even when errors occur, returning the last error among the send operations. An empty queue returns success. Calling queueMessage consumes the message, both on success and on error. Pass an existing object with std::move(outbound) or use a converter's result directly. Afterward, do not access, release, or requeue the message. OutboundMessage contains a raw pointer: std::move allows the destination to be moved, but does not automatically set the pointer to null in the original object. Copying the object does not duplicate the message either; therefore, do not pass copies of the same message to the queue. If you never pass a created message to the queue, release it exactly once with Release() . With the network and batcher initialized, and event , payload , connection , and connections defined by the application, typical usage after a converter is: auto outbound = lightnerx::communication::event2OutboundMessage(
    event,
    payload,
    lightnerx::network::Target::single(connection)
);

const auto queued = lightnerx::network::queueMessage(std::move(outbound));

if (!queued) {
    std::cout << "Failed to queue: " << queued.error().message() << '\n';
} else if (const auto flushed = lightnerx::network::flushAll(connections); !flushed) {
    std::cout << "Failed to send: " << flushed.error().message() << '\n';
} You can also queue multiple messages before calling flushAll . The batcher keeps each message separate, and the application chooses when to flush. Validation and callback behavior ¶ Both interpretation methods accept exactly one complete message. The declared total size must equal the supplied span and include the entire header. Authority sizes are decoded from 15 bits (maximum 32767 bytes); Obedient sizes use 16 bits (maximum 65535 bytes). Truncated headers, mismatched sizes, unknown response status and unregistered Obedient origins produce no callback, matching the existing void parser interface. There is no incremental or concatenated-message framing. Callbacks run synchronously. Registration during a callback is safe; newly registered callbacks participate from the next dispatch, including a nested interpretation. Existing callback objects retain mutable state. Receive/interpreter/ forEach exceptions propagate to their caller, with received buffers released even when a client batch is interrupted. Exceptions from GNS status callbacks are caught at the boundary and the first exception is rethrown by Server::update() after RunCallbacks() returns. A throwing callback ends its current callback sequence. Recursive update() returns operation_in_progress . Callbacks may disconnect or request Server::close() ; do not destroy or assign the component executing a callback. Referenced objects must remain alive until dispatch finishes. A negative receive result returns io_error , distinct from an empty queue; the client also clears and closes its failed connection. Updating an unstarted/closed wrapper returns not_connected . ConnectionManager owns an independent, bidirectional mapping; it neither reads nor writes GNS userdata. A handle may be registered once per manager. Adding a duplicate, invalid handle, or a handle beyond capacity returns nullopt . Different managers may independently register the same valid handle. getClientId() returns nullopt for an absent registration, including handle zero, and retains a registered identity after transport closure until removal. IDs range from 1 to 65535 and are recycled; negative capacities throw invalid_argument , capacity above 65535 is capped. forEach visits the index range present on entry, safely allowing add/remove; it may observe replacements within that range but does not extend traversal for vector growth. The batch queue is global. initMessagesBatcher() reserves capacity without discarding queued messages. clearMessagesBatcher() releases pending messages and preserves capacity; actual network shutdown and queue destruction also release pending messages. flushAll() releases every queued message if a send throws, then propagates the exception; with returned error codes it attempts all entries and returns the last error. Serialize all batch operations, including clearing and shutdown.

